must you behave so much like children so  I kind of want to talk a little bit  about something that comes up in the  comment section every now and then which  is that sometimes I will describe a  Doctor Who episode or an element in an  episode as being too silly and anytime I  do that I can be guarantee there's at  least gonna be a handful of comments of  people saying something to the effect of  Doctor Who's supposed to be silly or  you're okay with what goes on in this  episode but not this one how is this any  sillier or things of that nature  so I kind of want to address at least my  own assessment of Doctor Who's  relationship with being silly because  let's first openly acknowledge it is a  bit of a silly show and part of that has  to do with the nature of ridiculous  high-concept sci-fi premises they  honestly tend to be a bit silly and then  there's also the fact that it was  originally originally designed as a kids  show at this point I would consider a  family entertainment so there is an  element of that as well and often times  when writers can't think of another way  to do it they will get silly and  ridiculous as an appeal to a younger or  a family audience so silliness is kind  of baked into this at least to a certain  degree certainly now but I think that's  part of the thing I feel like the show  tries to be deliberately silly a bit  more now in modern whoo than it used to  because at least for the classic whoo  that I've taken in I'm still filling in  gaps it's gonna be a while until I've  seen all of classic who the thing is  though that most classic hood that I've  seen even at its silliest is presenting  it as serious whereas modern Doctor Who  not always but sometimes presents it  selling  as silliness and I think that's the  thing when I say that something is a bit  too silly I usually mean more the  execution rather than the concept  because pretty much all these concepts  are silly but by and large when it over  steps and it feels silly as opposed to  feels fun comes down to the execution  there are some things that are one-off  just ridiculousness and I can usually  roll with that assuming that it doesn't  like derail the whole thing like it  threw me off a little bit in the woman  who lived that the alien and that looked  like a Thundercat that threw me off a  little bit but you know whatever it's a  cat alien we've had cat aliens before  fine is it a little silly yes but it's  not so silly that it takes me out so I  think a good example for something where  it's presented as silly is love and  monsters because I think when silly  works assuming that it's more than just  like a joke like a single one-off joke  although even sometimes those can get  too silly I might get to that too is  it's not a is it works best when despite  the silliness the the show and the  characters had take this seriously  because often times there's a lot of  silliness put in that is inherent in the  villains the threat things like that so  the characters need to take that  seriously because if they act like the  threat is silly then what are the stakes  why do we care so coming back to loving  monsters you could certainly argue that  the characters you know in Lynda who are  you know the guy who's actually fleeing  the absorb all off is taking the series  because his life's in danger but that's  not enough because the way it's  presented  the way the absorb lava is played the  way he shot the way he lumbers in broad  daylight mm-hmm the way they have an  extended joke about what to even call  him the way the doctor kind of smirks  and snarks the fact that they the dr.  Andros only show up because this guy  irritated or annoyed roses mother not to  actually stop this thing it makes it  silly and incidental and in a way that  doesn't draw you in but in a way that  well nothing about this is being taken  seriously the show isn't taking it  seriously the heroes aren't taking it  seriously the monster doesn't seem to  take himself seriously and I'm not  saying that can never work in any medium  but that to me is too silly for Doctor  Who to work so not everybody does but I  personally quite liked the episode  robots of Sherwood and there's a lot of  silliness in that I mean it's got the  line I'm the doctor and this is my spoon  that's incredibly silly the way Robin  Hood behaves and his Merry Men Behaving  all is very silly but the way that that  causes things to happen in the episode  is it brings a bitter annoyance out of  the doctor so he's not just looking at  this and rolling his eyes and not taking  it seriously he's reacting to it and  then of course the threat is a  legitimate threat and the silliness in  that case is kind of separate from the  threat now I said when the show gets too  silly it's usually or when I feel it  gets too silly all this is opinion by  the way but if that wasn't clear alright  but when I say that it gets too silly I  usually mean this idea is too silly fear  her kind of falls under the same thing  to a certain degree in that case they  actually did take it quite seriously but  it that was a case where the inherent  idea of just a scribbled monster  well it felt like a goosebumps episode  the concept felt too silly and I suspect  for a lot of people they felt and I'm  projecting here because I actually liked  this episode but I think a lot of  people's issues with closing time is a  combination of the concept of Cybermen  under a mall being too silly coupled  with the inherent silliness of having  Craig back and again that one worked for  me but I suspect that's part of the  reason people don't like that but  sometimes there are specific moments  that themselves are too silly even if  the overall concepts aren't and I think  the best example of that is actually in  the very first episode of the revised  series it's in Rose and it's when the  trash bin eats Mickey and it's not it's  not silly because the CGI is wonky  because it's Doctor Who  yeah the effects are wonky sometimes and  the reason it's silly is because the  trash can burps what and that and that  is taking it because I mean classic who  would have played that as a horror  moment Mickey would have you know scream  aah and it just would have been and he's  gone and we're not entirely sure what  happened and that moment would not have  been played for comedy now you could say  it's so silly that they might as well  lean into it but for me having the thing  burp is it's a step too far  that's that is trying to hang a  lampshade on your inherent silliness and  if I use that phrase sometimes if you're  not familiar with the phrase the the  idea of lampshade hanging is that  something is off something is not  working but rather than fix it you draw  attention to it in a way that kind of  lets you off the hook for it so I mean  where the idea comes from is you know  you have this thing in you know in a  room that doesn't belong you just stick  a lampshade on it and it now you've now  drawn attention the fact that it's out  of place and people are like oh you made  a thing out of it okay  get it now that's sort of the idea of  lampshade hanging but the inherent  problem with lampshade hanging is you  didn't fix the problem you just got  weird and ironic and snarky about it and  you can't get away with that sometimes  but generally at least for me lampshade  hanging fails more often than it  succeeds because if something is so  ridiculous that you need to take that  approach to make it palpable maybe go  back to formula so that it's a little  less ridiculous in the first place so  that you don't have to make a joke out  of it in order for it to work so doctor  who is a silly show it will always be a  silly show it should be a silly show it  can also be a very dark and serious show  and I appreciate that but when it over  leans on it and another episode cut I've  got a lot of the worst examples for me  are in Davis's run and especially in  series 2 and I don't need to be picking  on Davies but I mean fair's fair he did  silly a lot more than Moffat did silly  and that's not me saying Moffat is  automatically better Moffat had his own  issues but being too silly was rarely  one of them being too complicated and  take himself too seriously that could be  a problem but you know that's opposite  side of the coin so with something like  the idiots Lantern I know people have  said that there that I I can't relate to  this because I'm American and I'm  prepared to accept that but I can only  speak from my perspective and from my  perspective the father in that episode  is so over-the-top blustering caricature  cartoonish that the whole thing becomes  silly rather than you know somebody oh  just going a little bit too far to  protect his reputation and his sense of  authority in the home it is played so  big that it becomes ridiculous it  becomes silly and in that case it  removes a fair amount of the stakes eh  he's soaked  to nurse there's to me no catharsis when  when people stand up to him because he's  ridiculous it he he feels unreal that's  what makes that silly in addition to the  fact that the wire does almost nothing  except golfy God the number of times she  repeats that I can't again it makes it  silly and I actually really like the  concept of the wire but the execution is  very very silly and sometimes silly work  silly works in doses I think oftentimes  when silly works best as when there's an  acknowledgment of the silliness of a  specific scenario a specific moment a  specific setting but probably best not  to have the villain the threat be silly  because that's kind of what I keep  coming back to because the the burping  trashcan that's supposed to be a threat  but it's made silly the scribble monster  supposed to be a threat adds to silly  the absorber lofts supposed to be a  threat it's played silly the wire and  that father  they're supposed to be threats they're  played silly and I just think that to  balance the elements of dark drew  because there's a lot of play in Doctor  Who because there is tinges of horror  and death and social commentary and  don't tell me like its new like I have a  whole video explaining why there's  always been social and political  commentary in this show but you know  there's there's that and there is a  playfulness there's all these elements  and balancing them is very very  difficult and I think at times things do  get so and they can get too silly it is  possible to be too silly it's possible  to be too serious it's possible to lean  too hard into the horror I would say  that that was part of what was wrong  with sleep no more to the point that  actually ate a trendy gimmicky horror  gimmick in terms of how it was shot so  any one of these elements can be over  leaned on but  none of them should be dropped I would  never argue get rid of all the silliness  and Doctor Who because it's part of the  identity of the show but it can't  overstep and for me when the show gets  too silly it's one of the more painful  things to have to sift through so for  some reason for me for all the over  steps and over emphasis that the various  elements of Doctor Who can sometimes get  from episode to episode over emphasizing  the silly just grates me a bit more than  over over emphasizing the emotional  dramatics the horror elements even the  political messaging which can get  heavy-handed and can be distracting but  ultimately I get more annoyed when I've  just filled this is too silly but that's  me this ramble had probably less of a  point even than I usually have talking  about Doctor Who but hey what are you  gonna do what are your thoughts what do  you think about when doctor who gets  silly what do you think are some of the  silliest moments of Doctor Who whether  they worked for you or not or are there  other things that doctor who has a show  sometimes overemphasizes that you think  is a bigger offense than being too silly  whatever your thoughts are drop  something down in the comments let's  talk about it there's all the stuff to  do you know what it is I've got a  patreon that's kind of a big one but  there are links to that and a whole  bunch of other stuff down in the  description so remember folks the  council is all of you I just run the  meetings and until next time this  council is adjourned  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]
 

